@node Naming system
@section Naming system

In Muddle, variable names use @code{case-insensitive} naming (conventionally @code{lower-case-with-hyphens} or @code{camelCase}).
All keywords are case-insensitive but conventionally written in lowercase.

@table @asis
@item Keywords: .if, .else, .then, .endif, .while, .endwhile, .for, .endfor, .exit, .return, .define, .set, .get, .put, .call, .go, .throw, .catch, .finally
@item Operators: +, -, *, /, ^, =, /=, <, >, <=, >=, and, or, not
@item String delimiters: Double quotes (@code{"..."}) or vertical bars (@code{||...||}) for literal atoms
@item Comments: Semicolon (;) to end of line
@end table

@node Basic structure
@section Basic structure for a stand-alone routine or program

A Muddle program consists of expressions and function definitions that are evaluated sequentially unless altered by control flow.

@example
;; Initialize game
(setq *score* 0)
(setq *lives-left* 3)
(setq *current-room* 1)

(defun initialize-game ()
  (setq *score* 0)
  (setq *lives-left* 3)
  (setq *current-room* 1))

(defun process-input ()
  ;; Simulate input handling
  (if (key-pressed? "right")
      (setq *current-room* (+ *current-room* 10))))

(defun update-game ()
  ;; Game logic updates)

(defun render-display ()
  ;; Rendering code)

(initialize-game)
(while (not *game-over*)
  (process-input)
  (update-game)
  (render-display))
@end example

@node Basic structure object
@section Basic structure for an object method

Muddle supports object-oriented programming through generic functions and methods (similar to CLOS).

@example
;; Define a Player class
(defclass player ()
  ((x :initarg :x :accessor player-x)
   (y :initarg :y :accessor player-y)
   (sprite :initarg :sprite :accessor player-sprite)))

;; Define a method for moving the player
(defmethod move-player ((p player) dx dy)
  (incf (player-x p) dx)
  (incf (player-y p) dy))

;; Create instance
(setq *player* (make-instance 'player :x 100 :y 100 :sprite 5))
@end example

@node Calling routines
@section Calling a routine (nullary)

To call a function that takes no arguments:

@example
(initialize-graphics)
(clear-screen)
(play-startup-music))
@end example

@node Calling routines value
@section Calling a routine with a value (in accumulator)

To call a function that returns a single value:

@example
(setq *score* (calculate-bonus *level* *time-left*))
(setq *bonus-factor* (get-input-factor))
(setq *volume* (get-master-volume))
@end example

@node Calling routines giving
@section Calling a routine giving a value (in accumulator)

Not applicable in Muddle's standard calling convention.

@node Calling routines values
@section Calling a routine with values in and out (in accumulator)

To call a function with parameters:

@example
(draw-sprite *sprite-id* *x-pos* *y-pos*)
(play-sound *sound-id* *volume* *loop-flag*)
(check-collision *obj1* *obj2* *result-var*)
@end example

@node Calling library
@section Calling a library routine (all 4 styles)

Muddle accesses library routines through standard function calls or method invocations:

@example
(load-level *level-number*)          @r{Direct call}
(far-proc *library-address*)         @r{Far call}
(setq *result* (library-function *input*))  @r{Library function}
(send *object* :method *param1* *param2*)   @r{Object method call}
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: FunctionName(...)
@item Far: AbsoluteAddress(...) (via external interface)
@item Library: LibraryEntry(...) or FunctionName(...)
@item Object: OBJECT:MethodName(...)
@end table

@node Calling far bank
@section Calling a far bank Service (all 4 styles)

Far procedures in Muddle are called using external interfaces or through
the foreign function interface:

@example
;; Define external interface
(external-call "farLoadLibrary" (address) -> void)

;; Call far routine
(external-call "farLoadLibrary" #x8000)   @r{Call routine in bank 1}
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Not used for far calls
@item Far: External interface call
@item Library: Not applicable
@item Object: Not applicable
@end table

@node Invoking object
@section Invoking a method on an object (all 4 styles)

Object method invocation in Muddle uses generic functions or send:

@example
(move-player *player* 5 10)
(send *score-display* :update *score*)
(send *game-state* :is-game-over)
@end example

These correspond to:
@table @asis
@item Direct: Setting object properties via slot accessors
@item Far: Not applicable for methods (objects typically in same memory)
@item Library: Method may call library routines internally
@item Object: METHOD invocation on object instance
@end table

@node Assignment
@section Assigning to a variable

Variables are assigned using setq or setf:

@example
(setq *score* 0)                     @r{Explicit assignment}
(setf *lives-left* 3)                @r{Implicit assignment}
(setf *counter* (1+ *counter*))      @r{Increment}
(setf *lives-left* (1- *lives-left*)) @r{Decrement}
(setf *score* (+ *score* 10))        @r{Arithmetic assignment}
(setq *message* "Hello, World!")     @r{String assignment}
@end example

@node Assignment object
@section Assigning to an object slot

Object slots are assigned using setf with accessors:

@example
(setf (player-x *player*) 160)       @r{Set object's x coordinate}
(setf (player-y *player*) 100)       @r{Set object's y coordinate}
(setf (player-sprite *player*) 5)    @r{Set sprite number}
(setf (player-visible *player*) t)   @r{Set visibility flag}
@end example

@node Assignment array
@section Assigning to an array member with a subscript

Muddle uses vectors for arrays. Elements are assigned using setf with accessor:

@example
(setq *score-list* (make-vector 10 :initial-element 0))
(setq *initials-list* (make-vector 10 :initial-element ""))
(setq *board* (make-array '(8 8) :initial-element 0))

(setf (aref *score-list* 0) 100)
(setf (aref *initials-list* 0) "ALI")
(setf (aref *board* 2 3) 1)
@end example

@node Arithmetic
@section Basic arithmetic: addition and subtraction

Basic arithmetic uses the + and - operators:

@example
(setq *score* (+ *score* 10))                  @r{Addition}
(setq *lives-left* (- *lives-left* 1))         @r{Subtraction}
(setq *total* (+ *width* *height))             @r{Add two variables}
(setq *final-price* (- *price* *discount*))    @r{Subtraction}
(setq *delta* (- *new-value* *old-value*))     @r{Subtraction}
@end example

@node Arithmetic bit
@section Basic arithmetic: bit-shifting for mutiplication or division

Bit-shifting for multiplication/division by powers of two is not directly supported.
Use ash (arithmetic shift) or external libraries:

@example
(setq *result* (ash *value* 2))                @r{Left shift 2 bits (multiply by 4)}
(setq *result* (ash *value* -1))               @r{Right shift 1 bit (divide by 2)}
(setq *result* (* *value* 4))                  @r{Multiply by 4}
(setq *result* (/ *value* 2))                  @r{Divide by 2}
(setq *result* (* *value* 8))                  @r{Multiply by 8}
(setq *result* (/ *value* 16))                 @r{Divide by 16}
@end example

@node Complex arithmetic
@section Complex arithmetic: multiple expressions

Complex arithmetic expressions follow standard precedence rules:

@example
(setq *total* (+ (* *base-salary* 12) *bonus* (- *deductions*)))
(setq *average* (/ (+ *sum1* *sum2* *sum3*) 3.0))
(setq *distance* (sqrt (+ (expt (- *x2* *x1*) 2) (expt (- *y2* *y1*) 2))))
@end example

@node Boolean
@section Boolean expressions: equality and inequalities

Boolean expressions use logical operators and comparison operators:

@example
(.if (> *score* 1000) ...)                 @r{Greater than}
(.if (< *score* 50) ...)                   @r{Less than}
(.if (= *score* 100) ...)                  @r{Equality}
(.if (/= *score* 0) ...)                   @r{Inequality}
(.if (and (>= *x* 0) (< *x* *max-x*)) ...) @r{Combined conditions}
(.if (or (= *button* *button-a*) (= *button* *button-b*)) ...) @r{Alternative conditions}
.endif
@end example

@node If then else
@section Using if/then/else structures and examine/cond structures

Conditional execution uses .if/.then/.else/.endif blocks:

@example
(.if (> *temperature* *thot*)
    (setq *status* "HOT")
  (.elseif (> *temperature* *tcold*)
    (setq *status* "WARM")
  (.else
    (setq *status* "COLD")
  .endif)

;; Muddle does not have a built-in case statement, but we can use .if/.elseif/.else/.endif
(.if (or (= *answer* "Y") (= *answer* "y"))
    (princ "YES")
  (.elseif (or (= *answer* "N") (= *answer* "n"))
    (princ "NO")
  (.else
    (princ "INVALID")
  .endif)
@end example

@node Performing
@section Performing loops

Loops are implemented using .while/.endwhile, .for/.endfor, and recursion:

@example
;; .for/.endfor loop equivalent (known iterations)
(.for *i* 1 10
    (princ "Count: " *i*)
  .endfor)

;; .while/.endwhile loop equivalent (pre-test)
(setq *i* 1)
(.while (<= *i* 10)
    (princ "Count: " *i*)
    (setq *i* (1+ *i*))
  .endwhile)

;; Recursive loop equivalent
(defun loop-until-quit ()
  (let ((command (read-input)))
    (if (string= command "quit")
        nil
        (progn
          (process-command command)
          (loop-until-quit)))))

;; .for/.endfor with exit
(.for *i* 1 100
    (if (= (vector-ref *array* *i*) *target-value*)
        (progn
          (princ "Found at index: " *i*)
          (.return)))   @r{Exit loop}
    (if (< (vector-ref *array* *i*) 0)
        (.continue))   @r{Skip to next iteration}
  .endfor)
@end example

@node Breaking loops
@section Breaking out of loops safely

Early loop exit is achieved using .return or .continue statements:

@example
;; Exit .for loop early
(.for *i* 1 100
    (if (= (vector-ref *array* *i*) *target-value*)
        (progn
          (princ "Found at index: " *i*)
          (.return)))   @r{Exit loop immediately}
  .endfor)

;; Skip current iteration
(.for *i* 1 100
    (if (< (vector-ref *array* *i*) 0)
        (.continue))   @r{Skip to next iteration}
  .endfor)

;; Nested loop control
(.for *i* 1 10
    (.for *j* 1 10
        (if (= (aref *matrix* *i* *j*) 0)
            (.return))   @r{Exit inner loop only}
      .endfor)
  .endfor)
@end example

@node Dialogue
@section Calling a dialogue script (Forth scripts)

Muddle does not directly call Forth dialogue scripts. Instead, it uses
its own functions. However, through the semantic-sugar
layer, Muddle commands can be mapped to calls of the shared Fountain/Forth
runtime library:

@example
;; Original Muddle code
(.if (and (> *x* 100) (< *x* 200))
    (show-message *msg-welcome*)
  .endif)

;; Desugared via semantic-sugar layer
(.if (and (> *x* 100) (< *x* 200))
    (:call-acc :target "ShowWelcomeMessage" :using nil)
  .endif)
@end example

@node Tiles
@section Manipulating tiles

Tile manipulation is performed through direct video memory access
or through library routines for tiled backgrounds:

@example
;; Direct memory access (mode-dependent)
;; Assuming direct memory access via pointer
(let* ((video-memory (make-pointer #xb8000 :type '(array unsigned-char)))
       (offset (* (+ (* *row* 80) *col*) 2)))
  (setf ( deref video-memory offset) *tile-num*)
  (setf ( deref video-memory (+ offset 1)) *attribute*))

;; Library routine approach
(load-tile-set *tile-set-id*)
(place-tile *layer* *x* *y* *tile-index*)
(scroll-background *dx* *dy*)
@end example

@node Decals
@section Manipulating decals

Decals (sprites or movable objects) are manipulated using:

@example
;; Hardware sprite approach (if available)
(init-sprites)
(set-sprite-position *sprite-id* *x* *y*)
(set-sprite-image *sprite-id* *image-id*)
(show-sprite *sprite-id* -1)   @r{-1 = visible}

;; Software sprite approach
(defclass sprite ()
  ((x :initarg :x :accessor sprite-x)
   (y :initarg :y :accessor sprite-y)
   (frame :initarg :frame :accessor sprite-frame)))

(setq *sprite* (make-instance 'sprite :x 100 :y 50 :frame 0))

(setf (sprite-x *sprite*) 100)
(setf (sprite-y *sprite*) 50)
(setf (sprite-frame *sprite*) (mod (1+ (sprite-frame *sprite*)) 4))
@end example

@node Objects
@section Manipulating objects

Object manipulation depends on the implementation approach:

@example
;; Using generic functions and methods (CLOS style)
(defclass game-object ()
  ((x :initarg :x :accessor game-object-x)
   (y :initarg :y :accessor game-object-y)
   (width :initarg :width :accessor game-object-width)
   (height :initarg :height :accessor game-object-height)
   (active :initarg :active :accessor game-object-active)))

;; Create instances
(setq *game-objects* (make-list 20 :initial-element (make-instance 'game-object
                                                                   :x 100 :y 100
                                                                   :width 16 :height 16
                                                                   :active t)))

;; Update object position
(dolist (go *game-objects*)
  (incf (game-object-x go) *dx*)
  (incf (game-object-y go) *dy*))

;; Check active status and draw
(dolist (go *game-objects*)
  (when (game-object-active go)
    (draw-object go)))
@end example

@node Unsupported
@section Unsupported language features

The following Muddle features are not supported in this EIGHTBOL release:

@table @asis
@item File I/O operations (open, read-line, write-string, close)
@item Graphics commands (draw-point, draw-line, draw-circle, fill-polygon)
@item Hardware-specific features (mouse, keyboard beyond basic keys)
@item Extended memory handling (unless emulated)
@item Multithreading or concurrent execution
@item Exception handling (unless via external interface)
@item Pointer arithmetic and manual memory management
@item Preprocessor directives (#define, #ifdef)
@item Object-oriented multiple inheritance (unless using external library)
@end table

